(BAS-57 - undocumented precision on BAS-41/BAS-51, and the repro behind
 Discrepancy 2: an `Otherwise` (or `But if`) still continues the innermost
 `if` ACROSS a blank line - even across the blank line that LANGUAGE.md:91
 says closes a function definition. Here the `Otherwise` block re-enters
 `check`'s body: it prints the parameter `n`, which only exists inside the
 function.
 Any intervening statement, by contrast, ends the chain and makes the
 `Otherwise` a compile error - see D2.vox.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox BAS-57.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   other 2
   one
   done
)
To check with a number called n.
    If n is equal to 1 then,
        print "one".

Otherwise,
    print "other {n}".

check of 2.
check of 1.
Print "done".
